  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 208 PM EDT Sat Oct 17 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis/Forecast: Mid-upper level heights are in the \nprocess of rising this afternoon across the western Great Lakes. \nZonal to weakly ridged upper levels expected across the area through \nat least the middle of next week. At the surface, expansive high \npressure is draped from the eastern Great Lakes south and westward \ninto the central and southern Plains. By later tonight through \nSunday, northern MI will lie on the northern periphery of this \n~1034mb high pressure that becomes centered to our south over the \nOhio Valley. Meanwhile, low pressure is expected to be crossing well \nto our north across parts of northern Saskatchewan/Manitoba/Hudson \nBay. As a result, a tightening pressure gradient is already in the \nprocess of ramping up locally, which will remain the case through \nSunday and result in renewed breezy to windy conditions. High \npressure largely expected to remain in control all the way through \nthe middle of next week before low-end shower chances return toward \nThursday of next week as a northern stream system looks to pass by \nto our north. \n\nForecast Details: Primary focus this weekend revolves around a \nreturn of breezy to gusty winds as northern MI becomes sandwiched \nbetween high pressure centered to our south and low pressure \ncrossing well to the north. Winds out of the southwest in the \nprocess of ramping up early this afternon, gusting as high as 20-30 \nmph through the remainder of today, and 25-40 mph at times on \nSunday. Sunday's upper end highest gusts likely to occur nearest the \nLake Michigan lakeshore of northwest lower into western and central \nMackinac County.  \n\nA warming trend begins Sunday and continues through much of next \nweek as Pacific-dominant flow pattern should allow temperatures to \nremain above normal for mid-October. Highs Sunday expected to range \nfrom the upper 50s far north and low-mid 60s for northern lower. \nMonday through the rest of the week largely in the 60s area-wide, \nwith probabilities increasing for lower 70s across parts of northern \nlower for Wednesday onward. Next low-end rain chances look to hold \noff until Thursday as a northern stream wave is progged to pass by \nto our north, however, latest trends suggest these chances are \ndiminishing as a whole.",
